{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792182246,"nonce":"uLcqJTVHxgQnzaNq2ivPRTq2UQU=","title":"Split images interleave names with bodies, so indexing the names touches every page","message":"serialize_module_split wrote each function's name, shell and body together. LinkIndex::of, built once per process for the embedded builtins image (1687 functions, about 2.5 MB in the executable's read-only data), reads every name, so it first-touched nearly every page of the image. A full-stack profile of method_call put 10 to 12 samples at 10 kHz (about 1 ms) in LinkIndex::of, nearly all of it leaf time in from_utf8 over the name slices (page faults).\n\nFix: write all names, then all shells, then all bodies. Extents still address the blob, so the format and its readers are unchanged.\n\nMac, whole process, warm-up off, 12 interleaved runs: empty.lua 50.72M -\u003e 49.88M instructions (-1.7%), mandelbrot_strip 100.56M -\u003e 99.91M; Python empty.py 66.22M -\u003e 65.97M (-0.4%), tiny.py 69.29M -\u003e 69.13M. Profiled, LinkIndex::of drops out of the top of the main thread. Wall moves within noise (empty.lua 7.7 -\u003e 7.6 ms over 20 runs; Python unchanged), so the saving is mostly kernel fault time on the first read.\n\nChecked: compiler bytecode unit tests, embed suite, 22 Python kernels against CPython, 17 Lua benchmarks against PUC Lua.","files":null}]}